### Describe the feature you'd like to request

This is based on #4190 

Currently we can create a typesafe input within a middleware like this:

<img src="https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/8896153/232254100-ed261297-e8ef-448f-8e20-4b049e2aae53.png" width=50% height=50%>

However the canonical way to create a re-usable and composable middleware is with `t.middleware`, not `procedure.use()`. 

When using the more composable approach we give up the type-safety:

<img src="https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/8896153/232254147-12bbc001-8c74-4989-bd5b-d7940f9b97f7.png" width=50% height=50%>

It would be a good step for composition to support type-safe inputs via `t.middleware`

### Describe the solution you'd like to see

There are a couple possibilities:

#### TypeScript interface

Allow `t.middleware` to take a generic parameter defining a mandatory input interface which must be met when it's used:

```ts
const orgMiddleware = t.middleware<{ orgId: number }>((opts) => {
      if (checkOrg(opts.input.orgId)) {
        // do checks and throw if needed

        return opts.next();
      }

      throw new TRPCError({
        code: 'FORBIDDEN',
      });
})

const scopedProcedure = publicProcedure
  .input(z.object({ orgId: z.number() }))
  .use(orgMiddleware)

const scopedProcedure = authProcedure
  .input(z.object({ orgId: z.number() }))
  .use(orgMiddleware)

// This would blow up at compile-time because the input type isn't valid
const scopedProcedure = otherProcedure
  .use(orgMiddleware)
```

#### Zod 'preprocess' style 


```ts
const orgMiddleware = t.middleware(
    // we pass a second property for the input parser, it will stack and merge just like .input does over multiple calls
    z.object({ orgId: z.number() }),

    (opts) => {
      if (checkOrg(opts.input.orgId)) {
        // do checks and throw if needed

        return opts.next();
      }

      throw new TRPCError({
        code: 'FORBIDDEN',
      });
    }
)

const scopedProcedure = publicProcedure
  .use(orgMiddleware)

const scopedProcedure = authProcedure
  .use(orgMiddleware)
```

### Describe alternate solutions

Alternatively we simply document a workaround for the issue, for instance this will provide the type safety to the client, and to the internals but is more verbose and doesn't enforce the use of the input :

```ts
const OrgObj = z.object({ orgId: z.number() })

const orgMiddleware = t.middleware((opts) => {
      const { orgId } = OrgObj.parse(opts.input)

      if (checkOrg(orgId)) {
        // do checks and throw if needed

        return opts.next();
      }

      throw new TRPCError({
        code: 'FORBIDDEN',
      });
})

const scopedProcedure = publicProcedure
  .input(OrgObj)
  .use(orgMiddleware)

const scopedProcedure = authProcedure
  .input(OrgObj)
  .use(orgMiddleware)

// This will very likely blow up at runtime and not provide the type safety we generally go for!
const scopedProcedure = otherProcedure
  .use(orgMiddleware)
```

